<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Package xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
    Retire the Disposition_Deal_Driver permission set. 2026-08-19.

    STATUS: AUTHORISED BY THE USER. This is the deliberate reason it is a SEPARATE folder
    from manifest/disposition-redesign-destructive/ rather than two more lines in that
    file. See "WHY THIS IS ITS OWN MANIFEST" below before consolidating them.

    DELETES EXACTLY ONE COMPONENT.

    1. PermissionSet Disposition_Deal_Driver
       Its entire payload was the Disposition_Deal_Actions CUSTOM PERMISSION. That custom
       permission is NOT retired and is NOT in this file: it still gates fifteen flexipage
       visibility rules and every server side assert in
       DispositionActionPermissionService. The grant has MOVED to DPEG_Disposition_Edit
       (see that file's own 2026-08-19 header block). Only the SET goes.

    🔴 POST destructive IS LOAD BEARING, NOT A DEFAULT. The replacement grant on
       DPEG_Disposition_Edit must be LIVE BEFORE this set is removed. Deleting first
       leaves a window in which NO user in the org holds Disposition_Deal_Actions, and the
       failure is SILENT in both halves of the gate: a flexipage visibility rule that
       evaluates false hides its button with nothing logged, and
       FeatureManagement.checkPermission simply returns false rather than throwing. Nobody
       gets an error; the buttons are just gone.

    ================================================================================
    WHY THIS IS ITS OWN MANIFEST
    ================================================================================

    manifest/disposition-redesign-destructive/destructiveChangesPost.xml already exists
    and already targets the same org in the same programme, so folding this in looks
    tidier. It is not, for one mechanical reason:

      A destructiveChanges file is deployed AS A UNIT. There is no flag that selects a
      subset of its types elements at deploy time.

    That file's two components (ValidationRule Wire_Complete_Before_Completed and
    PathAssistant Disposition_Path) are marked "PREPARED, NOT AUTHORISED - do not deploy
    without the user's explicit go ahead". Adding an AUTHORISED deletion to it would mean
    the only way to execute the authorised one is to execute all three, silently promoting
    two unauthorised deletions by side effect. Separate folders keep each authorisation
    state independently deployable, and neither file's status can be changed by acting on
    the other. The two components in that other file are UNAFFECTED by this one and remain
    unauthorised.

    ================================================================================
    🔴 PRE FLIGHT GATES. THIS DELETION IS NOT SAFE ON ITS OWN.
    ================================================================================

    1. APEX RESOLVES THIS SET BY NAME, AND A DELETED SET DOES NOT THROW A COMPILE ERROR -
       it returns zero rows. At the time this manifest was written, two test classes name
       the set in SOQL or in a constant:
         classes/BovSubmissionServiceTest.cls
         classes/DispositionActionPermissionServiceTest.cls
       A RunLocalTests deploy will FAIL on those until the parallel developer pass repoints
       them at DPEG_Disposition_Edit. Confirm that landed before running this. This repo
       has been bitten by exactly this: test SOQL is the blind spot in a permission set
       retirement, because nothing else in the codebase references a set by name.

    2. NO PermissionSetGroup CARRIES THIS SET. Verified by reading all five files in
       force-app/main/default/permissionsetgroups/ - it is direct assigned only, so no
       group member list needs editing and no group recalculation is pending on it.

    3. ASSIGNMENTS ARE ORG STATE AND ARE NOT MIGRATED BY ANY DEPLOY. A current
       Disposition_Deal_Driver assignee keeps the capability ONLY if they also hold
       DPEG_Disposition_Edit. Check that overlap in the org BEFORE running this, not after.

       🔴 CORRECTED 2026-08-19, MEASURED. This gate previously ended with the sentence
       "Deleting a permission set removes its assignments with it." THAT WAS FALSE and it
       cost a failed deploy. The platform does NOT cascade the assignments away: it REFUSES
       the delete outright while even one assignment exists. Dry run 0Afiw000000Mzo3CAC
       returned, as its only error:

         "This permission set is assigned to one or more users. You can only delete
          permission sets that aren't assigned to users. : Junior Dhanani."

       So retiring an ASSIGNED permission set is a TWO STEP job, and step one is not a
       deploy at all:

         a. Delete every PermissionSetAssignment row for the set. This is org DATA, not
            metadata. No deploy performs it, nothing in this repo expresses it, and it
            cannot be added to this file. Verify FIRST that the payload really is redundant
            (query SetupEntityAccess, FieldPermissions and ObjectPermissions by ParentId)
            so the assignee provably loses nothing.
         b. Only then run the destructive deploy below.

       ⚠ BOTH directions are manual. Removing the assignment is manual, and re-assigning on
       rollback is manual. See docs/permission-set-retirement-runbook.md for the exact
       command and for the note that an agent may be blocked from running it.

    ================================================================================
    EXECUTED 2026-08-19
    ================================================================================
    Assignment 0Paiw000000sIzUCAU (Junior Dhanani) removed as org data first, then deploy
    0Afiw000000N2nZCAS deleted the set. Test level NoTestRun, deliberately: this org carries
    three pre-existing failures unrelated to this change (ContactSelectorTest plus two in
    LeadFirstSeenValidationTest) which would have failed the deploy on foreign defects.
    DispositionActionPermissionServiceTest and BovSubmissionServiceTest were run separately
    afterwards: 17 of 17 passed. Readback confirmed the set gone, the custom permission
    Disposition_Deal_Actions still present, and its grantors down to exactly two:
    DPEG_Disposition_Edit and the DPEG_Junior_Analyst_PSG group aggregate.

    4. manifest/package.xml no longer lists this set. That was deliberate: while the set
       still exists in the org, a retrieve driven by that manifest would pull it back into
       force app and resurrect the file this change deletes.

    HOW TO RUN IT (only after gate 1 is confirmed and the additive payload has landed):
      run `sf project deploy start` with
        the manifest flag           pointing at this folder's package.xml
        the post destructive flag   pointing at this file
        the target org flag         set to the disposition org alias
        the test level flag         set to RunLocalTests, and a 30 minute wait.
      Validate with the dry run flag first. The flag prefixes are spelled out in words on
      purpose: an XML comment body cannot contain two consecutive hyphens, so writing the
      real flags here would make this file unparseable. That has bitten this repo twice.

    Rollback: the deleted file is recoverable from git history and can be redeployed, but
    RE ASSIGNMENT IS MANUAL - the assignments do not come back with it.
    -->
    <types>
        <members>Disposition_Deal_Driver</members>
        <name>PermissionSet</name>
    </types>
    <version>67.0</version>
</Package>
